// always(p) at suffix(i) means p holds at suffix(i).suffix(j) for all j
// always(p) at ex means p holds at ex.suffix(j) for all j
// So always(always(p)) implies always(p)?
// actually always(p).satisfied_by(ex.suffix(i)) means forall j, p.satisfied_by(ex.suffix(i).suffix(j))

